      ],
      "claim": "Definition problems in Minkowski theory are sometimes, not always, addressed using analytic continuation from Euclidean signature."
    },
    {
      "html_lines": [
        61,
        61
      ],
      "printed_pages": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "claim": "Minkowski noncommuting equation-of-motion operators and free Wightman distributional holomorphic boundary values contrast with commuting Euclidean fields, no equation of motion, and free Schwinger actual functions."
    },
    {
      "html_lines": [
        62,
        62
      ],
      "printed_pages": [
        2
      ],
      "claim": "Minkowski states can be covariantly defined; Euclidean construction chooses imaginary time and OS reflection to define state space and Lorentz-invariant inner product, perpendicular to the path-integral state hypersurface."
    },
    {
      "html_lines": [
        63,
        63
      ],
      "printed_pages": [
        2
      ],
      "claim": "Reverse Minkowski-to-Euclidean continuation has infinitely many Euclidean slices, individually chosen by imaginary time direction; SU(2,2) real form is determined by signature-(2,2) Hermitian Phi."
    },
    {
      "html_lines": [
        64,
        64
      ],
      "printed_pages": [
        2
      ],
      "claim": "Phi-null locus in PT is real dimension five; its projection to tau=0 S3 inside compactified S4 depends on imaginary time parameter choice."
    },
    {
      "html_lines": [
        65,
        65
      ],
      "printed_pages": [
        2
      ],
      "claim": "Minkowski physical states carry SL(2,C) simple group action; Euclidean Spin(4)=SU(2)L x SU(2)R is a two-simple-factor product and does not act on physical states in source's stated comparison."
    }
  ],
